---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/compare/recart-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Recart vs Postscript: Shopify SMS Recovery Compared';
const desc = "Recart vs Postscript for Shopify SMS: managed execution, campaign ownership, recovery handoffs, reply staffing and matched cost comparisons.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Recart or Postscript better for Shopify cart recovery?",
    "answer": "Neither is a universal recovery winner. Compare the same cart event, purchase exclusion and inventory change in each proposed setup. Recart’s managed service is relevant when execution capacity is missing; Postscript’s campaign and automation tools are relevant when your team wants to operate the program. Agree who owns changes before selecting either."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Recart replace Postscript for SMS marketing?",
    "answer": "Potentially, if the required campaigns, automations, destinations, consent records and reply routing are supported in the agreed package. Recart is currently positioned around SMS, not merely an old Messenger recovery use case. Map the active program before replacing its sender; a new integration alone does not transfer every queued message or permission record."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which handles camping equipment flash sale texts better?",
    "answer": "Use a proposed tent sale to compare audience exclusions, sold-out destinations and cancellation of a scheduled promotion. Ask the actual operator to change the featured variant after approval and show the revised preview. Score the handoff and ability to stop an unsuitable send rather than assuming either vendor wins a seasonal spike."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does pricing compare for 8,000 SMS sends monthly?",
    "answer": "Request quotes for the same destinations, message segments, carrier fees, service scope and contract period. Eight thousand campaign recipients are not necessarily eight thousand billable SMS segments. Add automated recovery traffic and any MMS separately. No fixed vendor price or recovered-order saving is assumed here."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I run Recart and Postscript together?",
    "answer": "Avoid overlapping recovery enrollment during a switch. A temporary split can be deliberate, for example disjoint migration cohorts, but each shopper and event needs one documented sending owner. Stopping an automation must include inspecting its queued messages; disabling a new trigger may leave older work pending."
  },
  {
    "question": "What about pairing with vinyl shop email drops?",
    "answer": "Keep long-form release notes and artwork in the chosen email system, with SMS reserved for its separately permitted audience and purpose. Before a limited pressing launch, decide whether a purchaser leaves both promotional queues and what happens when the pressing sells out. An email subscription should not be treated as SMS permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Recart messenger still matter in 2026?",
    "answer": "Do not choose Recart on an assumed legacy Messenger capability. Its current official positioning emphasizes managed SMS. If Messenger is essential, obtain a specific current channel and integration commitment rather than inferring it from older reviews. This page does not certify that channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "What buyer test settles Recart vs Postscript?",
    "answer": "Have the people who would run the account demonstrate one cart recovery, one sale campaign and one shopper reply using a proposed test audience. Include a purchase before send, a withdrawn permission and an unavailable product. Compare the resulting queue decisions and approval responsibilities; no timed pilot result is claimed."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Recart vs Postscript</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Recart vs Postscript for Shopify</h1><p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Choose an SMS operating model as well as a sending tool. Recart markets fully managed SMS; Postscript offers Shopify SMS campaigns, automation and reply-based workflows. This comparison focuses on who approves campaigns, handles shopper replies and stops recovery after a purchase. The examples below are proposed buyer exercises, not merchant results.</p></header>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>A managed SMS program versus operating the tools</h2>
        <p>Recart’s managed-service positioning changes the procurement question: specify the work being bought. Ask whether copy, campaign scheduling, automation maintenance and reporting are included in your agreement, and identify the merchant approver. Postscript’s SMS feature page describes campaigns, automations and reply-based follow-ups. That establishes product scope, not a promise that your team needs no implementation work. Neither description proves faster recovery or better compliance for your store.</p>
        <h2>A cart becomes an order while recovery is waiting</h2>
        <p>Proposed scenario: a shopper abandons a tent cart, then buys through a different visit before the reminder is due. Trace how that order becomes visible to the recovery owner and whether eligibility is evaluated again near send time. Repeat with a delayed or repeated cart event. Use a stable event or cart key to avoid enrolling the same opportunity twice; do not use order ID as the only key for a cart that has not yet converted. Record the remaining integration limitations.</p>
        <h2>A limited pressing sells out after campaign approval</h2>
        <p>For a proposed vinyl release, prepare the SMS destination and fallback before scheduling. A page that changes to sold out may still be useful if it clearly explains availability; a stale promise of stock is not. Have the operator show whether a queued message can be cancelled or edited and which preview reflects the final link. Do not assume that a product block refreshes inventory at send time merely because Shopify is connected.</p>
        <h2>Replies need a staffed destination</h2>
        <p>A question about tent capacity is different from a request to stop messages. Assign support replies to a staffed inbox with escalation rules, while honoring withdrawal through the supported unsubscribe mechanism. Demonstrate a reply arriving outside your team’s hours and show the next operator’s view. Automated reply branches may help route intent, but they do not supply a support team or establish the legal permission for a promotion.</p>
        <h2>Keep the email handoff explicit</h2>
        <p>An SMS program may sit alongside Sequenzy, Klaviyo or another email system, but that is a stack design choice rather than a claim that every integration is ready. Keep education and detailed product comparisons in email where appropriate. Write down which system starts recovery and how purchase or opt-out changes reach each channel. Set spacing from customer expectations, channel rules and the actual journey; there is no universal forty-five-minute safe interval.</p>
        <h2>Compare the same workload and service scope</h2>
        <p>Build a quiet-month and peak-month quote request from intended campaigns, recovery traffic, destinations and message lengths. Include carrier charges, minimum commitments, onboarding, service work and exit costs where applicable. A managed-service quote and a software-only quote are different purchases. Attributed revenue should not be treated as incremental profit: a customer might have bought without the reminder. If measuring lift, plan a suitable holdout and include discounts and message costs.</p>
        <h2>What would justify each choice</h2>
        <p>Recart belongs on the shortlist when the missing resource is ongoing SMS execution and its proposed service responsibilities fit your approval process. Postscript belongs on the shortlist when its Shopify SMS tools fit the operator’s campaign and reply workflows. Ask either provider how access, reporting exports and program changes work if the operator leaves. Keep an existing satisfactory sender unless a demonstrated capability or service gap justifies migration risk.</p>
        <h2>Evidence and scope</h2>
        <p>This is an editorial decision framework, not a hands-on benchmark. Current role descriptions come from the linked official product pages. Prices, contracted service tasks, supported destinations and account-specific integrations require separate confirmation before purchase.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://recart.com/fully-managed-sms">Recart managed SMS</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/features">Postscript SMS features</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump">Postscript vs SMSBump</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Selection framework</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Recart vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
